<%
// Reading key for the tables above. Both /results and /config-detail include
// this, so the ids referenced by their <th aria-describedby> attributes are
// defined exactly once per page.
%>
<details class="glossary">
  <summary>What these columns mean</summary>
  <p class="glossary-intro">
    Speeds are in <strong>tok/s</strong> — tokens per second. A token is about
    ¾ of a word, so 100 tok/s is roughly 75 words a second. Values that were
    never recorded read <span class="not-measured">not measured</span> rather
    than showing a zero.
  </p>
  <dl class="glossary-list">
    <dt>Prompt eval tok/s</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-prompt-eval">
      Prefill throughput — how fast the prompt itself is read before any
      answer starts. Matters most for long-context and RAG-style work.
    </dd>

    <dt>Gen tok/s</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-gen">
      Decode throughput — tokens produced per second once the answer is
      underway. The number most people mean by "tokens per second."
      Reported as <em>aggregate</em> throughput — the total across every
      parallel session, not the rate one session sees — so it is comparable
      across the concurrency sweep. Per-session rate falls as concurrency
      rises even while this climbs; the per-slot figure is kept in each run's
      raw log.
    </dd>

    <dt>tok/s per W</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-tokw">
      Generation throughput divided by the GPU power actually drawn during the
      run. Only present when power was measured.
    </dd>

    <dt>Context</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-context">
      The KV-cache budget the server was started with, not the number of
      tokens actually used. The benchmark runs use a <em>unified</em> KV cache,
      so this is one shared pool rather than a total carved into fixed
      per-slot shares — raising concurrency does not shrink each session's
      ceiling. See the <a href="/methodology">methodology</a>.
    </dd>

    <dt>Prefill</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-prefill">
      How many prompt tokens each request carried. The second axis of the
      sweep alongside context: prefill sets how much work the prompt-eval
      number is averaged over.
    </dd>

    <dt>Concurrency</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-concurrency">
      Parallel sessions served at once. 1 is single-stream latency, which
      suits llama.cpp's design; higher values measure aggregate throughput
      under continuous batching, which suits vLLM's. One concurrency level
      alone never tells the whole story.
    </dd>

    <dt>Quant</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-quant">
      How tightly the model's weights are compressed. Q8 and FP8 keep the most
      quality and need the most memory; Q4 and INT4 halve the memory and run
      faster, at some cost to output quality. llama.cpp uses GGUF quants
      (Q4_K_M, Q8_0); vLLM uses AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 — these are different formats,
      not interchangeable settings.
    </dd>

    <dt>VRAM</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-vram">
      Graphics memory in use during the run. A B70 and B65 each have 32 GB, so
      this is what decides whether a model fits on one card.
    </dd>

    <dt>Flash attention</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-flash">
      A memory-efficient attention kernel. When on, it lowers KV-cache
      pressure and usually raises long-context throughput.
    </dd>

    <dt>MTP</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-mtp">
      Multi-token prediction — a small draft model guesses several tokens
      ahead and the main model checks them in one pass, so answers arrive
      faster without changing what the model would have said.
    </dd>

    <dt>Tensor split</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-tensor-split">
      How the model's layers were divided across multiple GPUs.
    </dd>

    <dt>Stability</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-stability">
      Whether the run completed or crashed. A fast number from a build that
      falls over is not a fair win — crashed runs stay published rather than
      being quietly dropped.
    </dd>

    <dt>Trust</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-trust">
      Who stands behind the number.
      <strong>Measured here</strong> means it was run on this project's own
      hardware. <strong>Reproduced</strong> means a contributor sent it and it
      was re-run here with a matching result. <strong>Reported</strong> means
      it was submitted and reviewed, but not re-run — the conditions on the row
      are the contributor's account of them. All three are published; the label
      is there so you can weight them yourself.
    </dd>

    <dt>Verified</dt>
    <dd id="gloss-verified">
      The date this run was reviewed and published. Every result here,
      including the maintainer's own, goes through the same
      <a href="/submit">submission</a> and review path.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</details>
